Article 17

Protection of women includes: protection of the personal security of victims or witnesses;

physical and psychological threats;

confidentiality of the identity of victims and witnesses;

provision of testimony during the legal process which is regulated through protection mechanisms.

Protection for women victims also includes the provision of safe houses as temporary shelters to obtain a sense of security during the judicial process and/or outside the judicial process.

The provision of protection and safe houses shall be carried out by the Regional Government.

Further provisions on the mechanism for the protection of women as referred to in paragraph (2), and the provision of safe houses as referred to in paragraph (3), shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 49

The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 45 letter c, are carried out by participating in the protection of women and children victims of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination, either individually or in groups.

The role of the community as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, women and child protection institutions, social welfare institutions, community organizations, educational institutions, mass media, and the business sector.

The role of the community in the implementation of protection of women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by: providing information through socialization and education regarding the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing input in the formulation of policies related to the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

reporting to the authorities if violations of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination against women and children occur;

playing an active role in the process of rehabilitation and social reintegration for women and children victims of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

conducting monitoring and supervision of the Protection of Women and Children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing facilities and infrastructure in order to support the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

playing an active role by eliminating negative labeling of women and children victims of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination; and providing space for women and children to participate and express their opinions.

Article 7

The rights of women are as follows: to live, maintain life and improve their standard of living;

to live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously both physically and spiritually;

to enjoy a good and healthy living environment;

to live in a family and continue offspring in a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of laws and regulations;

to obtain work according to ability, conditions and fair and just wages;

specifically for women with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services;

to play an active role in the political and governmental fields in accordance with their potential and capabilities;

to obtain information and legal services;

to obtain services to increase their income; and to obtain other rights in accordance with their human dignity and based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 5

The objectives of implementing protection for women are: to ensure the fulfillment of women's rights;

to protect and provide a sense of security for women;

to maintain household integrity in order to realize a harmonious and prosperous family;

to ensure the fulfillment of women's rights;

to provide services to women;

to provide protection to reporters and witnesses; and to empower women victims of violence.

CHAPTER II

SCOPE

Article 2

The Scope of this Regional Regulation consists of: implementation of women's protection; and implementation of child protection;

obligations and responsibilities;

institutions;

child-friendly regency;

special musrembang for women and children.

Article 46

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 45 letter a, include: establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;

conducting cooperation with other parties in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity;

fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing recovery services and social reintegration;

encouraging and increasing community participation; and providing human resources in the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination.

In the event that a child becomes neglected as referred to in Article 22, the Regional Government is responsible and obligated to take over and provide services to neglected children as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (2) letter a.

Community institutions, and/or individuals may submit applications to the court to become guardians and/or foster parents for neglected children.

Article 15

